tail:
    {
      "id": "thm-froda",
      "concern": "The 'More precisely' paragraph, under the hypothesis that f is monotone in either direction, calls (lim-left f, lim-right f) 'a nonempty open interval' at every interior discontinuity. For the nonincreasing step f(x)=1 for x<0 and f(x)=0 for x≥0, that interval at 0 is (1,0), hence empty. Proof 1.1 correctly reduces to −f, but the additional assertion in the Statement does not carry that reduction.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-continuity-iff-oscillation-zero",
      "concern": "The explanatory paragraph after the equivalence says 'the sets {x:ω_f(x)≥ε} are closed', although the item allows arbitrary A⊆ℝ. They are relatively closed, as the cited lemma explicitly states. For A=(0,1) and the restricted Dirichlet function, the superlevel set at ε=1 is (0,1), which is not closed in ℝ. Also L4 attributes the triangle inequality to lem-of-abs-value, whose Statement and proof do not establish it; the separate opened supplier is lem-of-triangle-inequality.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-semicontinuity-level-set-characterisation",
      "concern": "The first Remark says 'S_α is a subset of A, so it cannot be open in ℝ unless A is'. This is false. Take A=[0,1] and f≡0: for α=0, S_α=∅ is open although A is not. The intended statement that relative openness need not imply ambient openness is correct, as are the theorem and proof.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-hamel-basis-additive-function",
      "concern": "The first Remark says 'The proof uses nothing about f except that it is additive and not linear; every other property is read off thm-cauchy-functional-equation-regularity by contraposition.' This is false for claim 4. Rational range and dense level sets use the specific coefficient-map structure and nontrivial kernel in steps 2.2–2.3 and 3.3–4.1. Additive nonlinear maps can be injective: under the same AC assumption, swap two vectors of a Hamel basis and fix the others. The resulting rational-linear automorphism is nonlinear over ℝ and has singleton fibres.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "ex-cantor-function-maps-a-null-set-onto-the-unit-interval",
      "concern": "The first Remark unconditionally says 'Measure zero is preserved by countable unions' and cites thm-countable-union-of-null-is-null. That published supplier explicitly begins 'Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice'. The example does not assume it. The cited general closure claim needs that qualification under the library's choice bookkeeping; the actual Cantor-image example does not use the closure claim.",
      "severity": "gap-a-reader-closes"
    },
    {
      "id": "thm-cauchy-functional-equation-regularity",
      "concern": "The first Remark explains the separate graph argument by saying 'a non-dense graph gives no bound on f anywhere'. Literally this contradicts the proved implication: an additive function with nondense graph is linear, hence bounded on every bounded interval. The proof establishes density contrapositively without first deriving a bound; that distinction should replace the categorical wording.",
      "severity": "presentation"
    }
  ],
